---
layout: layouts/tool.njk
toolSlug: profit-margin-calculator
title: "Profit Margin Calculator: Margin vs Markup"
description: "Find profit, margin and markup from cost and price, or the selling price for a target margin or markup. Clear margin vs markup table included."
permalink: /tools/profit-margin-calculator/
h1: "Profit Margin & Markup Calculator"
extraCss: "/tools/profit-margin-calculator/style.css"
quickAnswer: "Margin is profit as a percentage of the selling price; markup is profit as a percentage of the cost. An item that costs 60 and sells for 100 makes 40 profit, a 40% margin and a 66.67% markup. To hit a target margin, price = cost ÷ (1 − margin), so a 25% margin on a cost of 75 needs a price of 100."
formula:
  title: "Margin and Markup Formulas"
  expression: "Profit = Price − Cost   ·   Margin % = Profit ÷ Price × 100   ·   Markup % = Profit ÷ Cost × 100"
  explanation: "Margin and markup use the same profit but divide it by different numbers, so markup is always the larger percentage. To find a price from a target margin, divide the cost by (1 − margin ÷ 100). To find a price from a markup, multiply the cost by (1 + markup ÷ 100). The two convert directly: markup = margin ÷ (1 − margin), and margin = markup ÷ (1 + markup), with both written as decimals."
  variables:
    - name: "Cost"
      description: "What the item costs you: purchase price, or materials and labour for things you make."
    - name: "Price"
      description: "The selling price before sales tax or VAT."
    - name: "Margin"
      description: "The share of the selling price you keep as gross profit. Always below 100%."
    - name: "Markup"
      description: "How much you add on top of the cost, as a percentage of the cost. Can be above 100%."
workedExamples:
  - title: "Cost 60, price 100"
    inputs:
      - "Cost: 60"
      - "Selling price: 100"
    steps:
      - "Profit: 100 − 60 = 40"
      - "Margin: 40 ÷ 100 = 40%"
      - "Markup: 40 ÷ 60 = 66.67%"
    result: "Profit 40, margin 40%, markup 66.67%"
    link: "/tools/profit-margin-calculator/?mode=price&cost=60&price=100&cur=USD#tool"
  - title: "Price for a 25% margin on a cost of 75"
    inputs:
      - "Cost: 75"
      - "Target margin: 25%"
    steps:
      - "Price = 75 ÷ (1 − 0.25) = 75 ÷ 0.75 = 100"
      - "Check: profit 25 ÷ price 100 = 25% margin"
    result: "Selling price 100 (a 33.33% markup)"
    link: "/tools/profit-margin-calculator/?mode=margin&cost=75&margin=25&cur=USD#tool"
  - title: "Price for a 25% markup on a cost of 80"
    inputs:
      - "Cost: 80"
      - "Markup: 25%"
    steps:
      - "Price = 80 × (1 + 0.25) = 100"
      - "Margin: profit 20 ÷ price 100 = 20%"
    result: "Selling price 100 (a 20% margin)"
    link: "/tools/profit-margin-calculator/?mode=markup&cost=80&markup=25&cur=USD#tool"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Adding the margin percentage to the cost"
    description: "Adding 25% to a cost of 75 gives 93.75, which is a 25% <em>markup</em> and only a 20% margin. For a 25% margin you must divide: 75 ÷ 0.75 = 100."
  - title: "Using margin and markup as the same thing"
    description: "A 50% markup is a 33.3% margin, and a 50% margin is a 100% markup. Say which one you mean in price lists, supplier negotiations and commission plans."
  - title: "Expecting a margin above 100%"
    description: "Margin is a share of the selling price, so it can never reach 100% unless the cost is zero. If a figure above 100% appears, it is a markup."
  - title: "Leaving costs out"
    description: "Gross margin uses the direct cost of the item. Shipping, payment fees, packaging and returns are real costs too; include them in cost if you want the margin you actually keep."
  - title: "Including sales tax or VAT in the price"
    description: "Tax collected for the government is not revenue. Use the selling price before tax, or the margin will look higher than it is."
faqs:
  - q: "What is the difference between margin and markup?"
    a: "Both start from the same profit (price − cost). Margin divides it by the selling price; markup divides it by the cost. Cost 60 and price 100 give a 40% margin but a 66.67% markup."
  - q: "How do I calculate profit margin?"
    a: "Subtract the cost from the selling price to get profit, divide the profit by the selling price, and multiply by 100. Price 100 and cost 60: (100 − 60) ÷ 100 = 40%."
  - q: "How do I calculate a selling price from a desired margin?"
    a: "Divide the cost by (1 − margin as a decimal). For a 25% margin on a cost of 75: 75 ÷ 0.75 = 100."
  - q: "How do I convert markup to margin?"
    a: "Margin = markup ÷ (1 + markup), using decimals. A 25% markup is 0.25 ÷ 1.25 = 20% margin; a 100% markup is a 50% margin."
  - q: "What is a 50% markup as a margin?"
    a: "A 50% markup is a 33.33% margin. Cost 100 marked up 50% sells for 150, and 50 profit ÷ 150 price = 33.33%."
  - q: "Can profit margin be negative?"
    a: "Yes. If you sell below cost, profit is negative and so are the margin and markup. The calculator shows this as a loss."
  - q: "What is a good profit margin?"
    a: "It depends on the industry, the business model and whether you mean gross or net margin. Compare with businesses like yours and include all your costs before deciding on a target."
sources:
  - name: "IRS Publication 334: Tax Guide for Small Business (gross profit and cost of goods sold)"
    url: "https://www.irs.gov/publications/p334"
  - name: "U.S. SEC: Beginners' guide to financial statements (gross profit and margins)"
    url: "https://www.sec.gov/about/reports-publications/beginners-guide-financial-statements"
  - name: "GOV.UK: VAT rates (exclude tax from selling prices)"
    url: "https://www.gov.uk/vat-rates"
lastReviewed: "2026-10-31"
howto:
