    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Margin vs Markup at a Glance</h2>
      <p>Margin and markup describe the same profit from two directions. Margin answers "how much of each sale do I keep?", while markup answers "how much did I add to my cost?". Because the selling price is always bigger than the cost when you make a profit, the margin percentage is always the smaller of the two.</p>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table class="table">
          <caption>Margin and markup for an item that costs 100</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Selling price</th><th scope="col">Profit</th><th scope="col">Margin (of price)</th><th scope="col">Markup (of cost)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>111.11</td><td>11.11</td><td>10%</td><td>11.11%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>125.00</td><td>25.00</td><td>20%</td><td>25%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>133.33</td><td>33.33</td><td>25%</td><td>33.33%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>150.00</td><td>50.00</td><td>33.33%</td><td>50%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>166.67</td><td>66.67</td><td>40%</td><td>66.67%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>200.00</td><td>100.00</td><td>50%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>300.00</td><td>200.00</td><td>66.67%</td><td>200%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>400.00</td><td>300.00</td><td>75%</td><td>300%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Read across a row to convert: a 25% markup is a 20% margin, and a 50% margin needs a 100% markup. As the margin approaches 100%, the markup grows without limit, which is why margins above 100% are impossible while markups of 200% or 300% are common for low-cost items.</p>

      <h2>Which One Should You Use?</h2>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Setting prices from costs:</strong> markup is quick for a price list ("cost plus 40%"), but always check the margin it gives you.</li>
        <li><strong>Planning and reporting:</strong> margin matches how financial statements report gross profit, and lets you compare products with very different prices.</li>
        <li><strong>Discounts:</strong> a discount comes off the selling price, so it eats into margin directly. A 20% discount on a product with a 20% margin wipes out the whole profit.</li>
        <li><strong>Talking to suppliers and staff:</strong> say "margin" or "markup" explicitly; "40% profit" is ambiguous.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This calculator works out gross margin on a single item: selling price minus the direct cost of that item. Net margin also subtracts overheads such as rent, wages, marketing and tax, so it is always lower than gross margin.</p>
